<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Common Causes of Website Downtime</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Website downtime strikes for various reasons and understanding them helps prevent future headaches. One frequent trigger involves server overload during traffic spikes, when too many visitors hit the site at once, causing slow response time or full inaccessibility.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Hosting provider choices play a big role too. Budget options like free WordPress hosting often lack redundancy, making them prone to crash under pressure. Even solid plans face issues from routine maintenance, DDoS attack, or a hacker exploiting weak security measures.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">In WordPress, incompatible plugins, outdated themes, or coding errors commonly lead to your site going down. Accidental deletion of files or bad DNS record changes block access. External factors like APIs failing or firewall or security settings blocking traffic add to the list.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Sometimes server errors stem from hardware failure or network problems at the host. These can make the website you are trying to load show error messages instead of content.</p>



