<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Common Problems Causing the Critical Error in WordPress</h2>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Plugin Conflicts and Updates Gone Wrong</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">One of the most frequent reasons for there has been a critical error on this website wordpress is a bad plugin interaction. When you update a plugin, it might not work well with other WordPress plugins or the current PHP version. Sometimes a single faulty plugin can trigger a critical error that brings down the whole WordPress site.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">You may also notice related issues like publishing failed the response is not a valid json response or updating failed the response is not a valid json response when trying to save posts. These messages point straight to plugin problems. In severe cases, the critical error message blocks access to your WordPress admin, leaving you unable to manage content. We often find that <a href="https://www.arwebcrafts.com/wordpress-plugin-development/">rolling back a recent plugin update</a> or disabling the conflicting plugin folder through FTP resolves the issue quickly.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Theme Issues and Incompatibilities</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Your WordPress theme plays a big role too. An outdated or poorly coded theme can cause critical errors after a WordPress core update. The error message might appear only on the front end, while the WordPress dashboard stays accessible, or the opposite can happen.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Sometimes the appearance navigation menu is missing in WordPress because the theme files got corrupted during an update. This incompatibility can trigger the error and make the site look broken. <a href="https://www.arwebcrafts.com/wordpress-development-agency-modern-web-development/">Switching away from the active theme</a> usually helps identify if the theme is the culprit behind the critical error in WordPress.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">PHP Version Mismatches and Memory Limits</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">WordPress requires a compatible version of PHP and running an older version of PHP is a common reason sites crash with there has been a critical error on this website wordpress. When WordPress updates, it often needs newer PHP features and the site stops loading if the server hasn&#8217;t kept up.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Another related problem is when WordPress sets a low PHP memory limit. Heavy plugins or large media files exhaust available memory, leading to a fatal error. You might see HTTP error 500 WordPress, WordPress 500 internal server error, or 500 internal server error WordPress instead of the standard message. To fix the problem, you often need to increase the PHP memory limit or update your PHP version through your hosting control panel.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Database Errors and Corrupted Files</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Corrupted WordPress files or database issues can create an error establishing a database connection or a full critical error on your website. This happens when core WordPress files get damaged during uploads via FTP or when the database tables break.&nbsp;A sudden server hiccup or failed automatic update leaves files incomplete. The result is a broken website that refuses to load. These problems are harder to spot without checking the error log.</p>



